#{[17592186396571 :question/worked-solution "A differential equation of the form\n\n$$\nP(x,y)\\text{d}x+Q(x,y)\\text{d}y=0\n$$\n\nis exact if it passes the exactness test, i.e.,\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}.\n$$\n\nIn our case, we're given $Q(x,y)=x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xy).$ \n\nFirst, let's differentiate $Q(x,y)$ with respect to $x$:\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=\\frac{∂}{∂x}(x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xy))=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xy)-xy\\operatorname{sin}⁡(xy)\n$$\n\nNow, let's apply $\\frac{∂}{∂y}$ to each of the given options:\n\n- First, we check $P(x,y)=y\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xy)-y.$ This gives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂}{∂y}(y\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xy)-y)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xy)-xy\\operatorname{sin}⁡(xy)-1\\ne \\frac{∂Q}{∂x}\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $P(x,y)=y\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xy)-y$ does *not* give an exact equation.\n\n- Next, we check $P(x,y)=xy\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xy)$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂}{∂y}(xy\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xy))=x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xy)-{x}^{2}y\\operatorname{sin}⁡(xy)\\ne \\frac{∂Q}{∂x}\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $P(x,y)=xy\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xy)$ does *not* give an exact equation.\n\n- Finally, we check $P(x,y)=y\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xy)$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂}{∂y}(y\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xy))=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xy)-xy\\operatorname{sin}⁡(xy)=\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $P(x,y)=y\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xy)$  gives an exact equation.\n\nHence, the correct answer is ''III only''."] [17592186396573 :answer/value "ÍII only"] [17592186404482 :question/worked-solution "A differential equation of the form\n\n$$\nP(x,y)\\text{d}x+Q(x,y)\\text{d}y=0\n$$\n\nis exact if it passes the exactness test, i.e.,\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}.\n$$\n\nIn our case, we're given $Q(x,y)=5{y}^{4}{e}^{2x}.$ \n\nFirst, let's differentiate $Q(x,y)$ with respect to $x$:\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=\\frac{∂}{∂x}(5{y}^{4}{e}^{2x})=10{y}^{4}{e}^{2x}\n$$\n\nNow, let's apply $\\frac{∂}{∂y}$ to each of the given options:\n\n- First, we check $P(x,y)=10{y}^{5}{e}^{x}+4.$ This gives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂}{∂y}(10{y}^{5}{e}^{x}+4)=50{y}^{4}{e}^{x}\\ne \\frac{∂Q}{∂x}\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $P(x,y)=10{y}^{5}{e}^{x}+4$ does *not* give an exact equation.\n\n- Next, we check $P(x,y)=2{y}^{5}{e}^{2x}-y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂}{∂y}(2{y}^{5}{e}^{2x}-y)=10{y}^{4}{e}^{2x}-1\\ne \\frac{∂Q}{∂x}\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $P(x,y)=2{y}^{5}{e}^{2x}-y$ does *not* give an exact equation.\n\n- Finally, we check $P(x,y)=2{y}^{5}{e}^{2x}+{e}^{-x}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂}{∂y}(2{y}^{5}{e}^{2x}+{e}^{-x})=10{y}^{4}{e}^{2x}=\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $P(x,y)=2{y}^{5}{e}^{2x}+{e}^{-x}$ gives an exact equation."] [17592186594690 :question/problem "$$\n\\displaystyle \\int {y}^{′}(t)\\,\\text{d}t=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\n$${{field-1}}\n$)+\\,K,$\nwhere $K$ is an arbitrary constant."] [17592186594690 :question/worked-solution "In part 9, we found that\n\n$$\n-4\\int \\frac{t}{{t}^{2}+3}\\,\\text{d}t=-2\\operatorname{ln}⁡({t}^{2}+3)+K.\n$$\n\nUsing the sum and constant multiple rules, we compute the sum of the integrals of each function in the partial sum decomposition of ${y}^{′}(t)$ found in part 8:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int {y}^{′}(t)\\,\\text{d}t & =\\int (\\frac{1}{t}-\\frac{4t}{{t}^{2}+1})\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\int \\frac{1}{t}\\,\\text{d}t-4\\int \\frac{t}{{t}^{2}+3}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡|t|-2\\operatorname{ln}⁡({t}^{2}+3)+K \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡t-\\operatorname{ln}⁡(({t}^{2}+3)^{2})+K \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\left(\\frac{t}{({t}^{2}+3)^{2}}\\right)+K.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186601926 :question/problem "$$\n\\displaystyle \\int {y}^{′}(t)\\,\\text{d}t=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\n$${{field-1}}\n$)+\\,K,$\nwhere $K$ is an arbitrary constant."] [17592186677832 :question/problem "Find the antiderivative:\n\n$\\displaystyle\\int \\sqrt[4]{t}-t^{4}\\,\\mathrm{d}t=$\n{{field-1}}\n$+C$. Assume $t>0$."] [17592186677834 :question/problem "Find the antiderivative:\n\n$\\displaystyle\\int -t^{2}-\\sqrt[4]{t}\\,\\mathrm{d}t=$\n{{field-1}}\n$+C$. Assume $t>0$."] [17592186681140 :question/problem "Calculate $\\displaystyle\\int -6\\sqrt{t}-4t^{2}-3\\,\\mathrm{d}t$. Assume $t>0$."] [17592186694428 :question/problem "Calculate $\\displaystyle\\int -6+x^{2}\\,\\mathrm{d}x$."] [17592186694436 :question/problem "Calculate $\\displaystyle\\int -y^{3}-2\\,\\mathrm{d}y$."] [17592186694450 :question/problem "Calculate $\\displaystyle\\int x^{4}+1\\,\\mathrm{d}x$."] [17592186694457 :question/problem "Calculate $\\displaystyle\\int -t^{4}+2\\,\\mathrm{d}t$."] [17592186694460 :question/problem "Calculate $\\displaystyle\\int x^{3}+4\\,\\mathrm{d}x$."] [17592186694585 :question/problem "Calculate $\\displaystyle\\int -1+x-3x^{2}\\,\\mathrm{d}x$."] [17592186694590 :question/problem "Calculate $\\displaystyle\\int 3+t-t^{2}\\,\\mathrm{d}t$."] [17592186694596 :question/problem "Calculate $\\displaystyle\\int -3-3x-2x^{2}\\,\\mathrm{d}x$."] [17592186694602 :question/problem "Calculate $\\displaystyle\\int -3-2t^{2}+2t\\,\\mathrm{d}t$."] [17592186704829 :question/problem "Calculate $\\int {x}^{8}\\,\\text{d}x.$ Use $C$ for the arbitrary constant of integration.\n\n{{antiderivative}}"] [17592186704833 :question/problem "Calculate $\\int {x}^{15}\\,\\text{d}x.$ Use $C$ for the arbitrary constant of integration.\n\n{{antiderivative}}"] [17592186704837 :question/problem "Calculate $\\int {x}^{1}\\,\\text{d}x.$ Use $C$ for the arbitrary constant of integration.\n\n{{antiderivative}}"]}
